{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## FRESH HARNESS RUN 30834117797 — the verdicts, on main @ 25ead49f3e (WITH 1296)\n\nJob `91755000385`. **`[jira-live-canary] collected=58 executed=58 skipped=0`** — the count the\nprevious comment predicted (58, up from 56), and `skipped=0` means every cell actually EXECUTED\nrather than silently skipping for want of a harness. **6 failed, 52 passed in 2022s.**\n\nThis run POSTDATES all three test-repair merges (1279/1280/1283), so unlike run 30784592232 its\nverdicts are readable against the repaired cells. It PREDATES 1302/1303.\n\n### The six reds, each against what this ticket predicted\n\n 1. **`test_outbound_create_stamps_both_provenance_markers` — FAILED, and it is the NEW row-1\n    cell failing exactly where its author predicted.** Verbatim: *\"the outbound pass did not\n    create-and-bind '654e-8c11-d632-4a01' (get_jira_key returned None), so there is no DC issue to\n    read the provenance markers off. Row 1's markers are written INSIDE the create\n    (`dispatch_one.py:306-307`), so a missing binding is upstream of this oracle, not a marker\n    finding.\"* The cell did its job: it localised the failure to the CREATE-and-bind step rather\n    than blaming the markers. **This is a genuine unresolved finding, not a test defect** — an\n    outbound create against DC did not produce a binding. Filed separately rather than absorbed\n    here, because it is a product question (see the new bug linked below).\n 2. **`test_inbound_delete_link_round_trips` — FAILED, as predicted, and NOT to be inverted.**\n    Verbatim: the Jira link was deleted but the local dep still targets `jira-rbjskzw-2`; *\"The\n    inbound link differ is ADD-only (`inbound_differ.py:380,396`) — see 2b16.\"* This is precisely\n    the cell change **1303** xfails with its reason. The red here is the evidence that 1303 is\n    warranted rather than cosmetic.\n 3. **`test_inbound_set_subtask_parent_round_trips` — FAILED on a 90s/45-attempt timeout** waiting\n    for `get_parent_map` to reflect a reparent. The cell's own message says this is *\"NOT a bridge\n    defect — the write succeeded, the search-backed parent map cannot see it yet\"*. Read together\n    with (6) below, I do NOT accept that self-diagnosis at face value; see the note under (6).\n 4. **`test_inbound_clear_parent_round_trips` — SETUP FAILED, and it reported it DISTINCTLY.**\n    Verbatim: *\"SETUP FAILED (not a rebar defect): Data Center REFUSED to clear the parent of\n    sub-task RBJYRMS-2 — BackendHTTPError: HTTP Error 400\"*. This is the PLATFORM finding 88d9\n    predicted, arriving in the distinct shape the cell was rebuilt to produce instead of a bare red.\n 5. **`test_outbound_clear_parent_round_trips` — FAILED**, which is 39c1. Expected: this run does\n    not carry 1302.\n 6. **`test_outbound_set_subtask_parent_round_trips` — FAILED, and this one was NOT predicted.**\n    Verbatim: *\"fields.parent on RBJUUVX-3 is STILL 'RBJUUVX-1' — the parent it had BEFORE the\n    mutation. Expected 'RBJUUVX-2'. This is the silent-no-op signature.\"*\n\n### THE FINDING THIS RUN ADDS, and it bears on 39c1\n\nCells (3) and (6) are both the SUB-TASK parent path, in the two directions, and both show the\nparent not moving. 39c1's mechanism analysis rests on the premise that **the sub-task shape is the\none DC accepts** (`transport.py:711-712` writes `fields.parent` for a sub-task, and the epic case\nis what declines). This run says the sub-task write did not take effect either — the outbound cell\nread the field back and found the ORIGINAL parent.\n\nThat makes (3)'s \"the write succeeded, the index is just lagging\" self-diagnosis the less likely of\nthe two readings: (6) read `fields.parent` back DIRECTLY, not through the search index, and still\nsaw the old value. A search-index lag cannot explain a direct field read. The likelier shared root\nis that **DC 8.17.1 accepts and ignores a sub-task reparent via `PUT fields.parent`** — the same\naccept-and-ignore shape `transport.py:688-689` is already flagged for on the CLEAR path.\n\nI am NOT closing that here. It is filed as its own bug so it is not buried in a story's comment\nthread, and because it changes what 39c1's AC1 can honestly claim."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785779162008919001,"uuid":"bdcd7d2b-735d-45fa-a214-edb286b13ab9"}